  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Consider the function $f:\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}$ where $f(n)$ is the number of positive divisors of $n$. Prove that $f$ is surjective.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f$ is surjective.\n\nFor every $m\\in\\mathbb{N}$, the number $2^{m-1}$ has exactly $m$ positive divisors, so $f(2^{m-1})=m$.",
      "worked": "Let $m\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and choose $n=2^{m-1}$. Its positive divisors are precisely $2^k$ for $k=0,1,\\ldots,m-1$, so there are $m$ of them. Thus\n$$f(2^{m-1})=m.$$\nEvery element of the codomain has a preimage in $\\mathbb{N}$. Therefore, $f$ is surjective.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
